From 0f27db5bfb637fb993dc75cc9f610b378f2fc734 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 06:17:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf: periodic kernels map each periodic coordinate onto
 a circle once per point

Along a periodic dimension the kernels computed the squared chord of every
pair of inputs, with an fmod and a sine over the whole N x M matrix, one
periodic dimension at a time, beside one cdist for the other dimensions,
and a fit's gradient computed each periodic dimension's chord a second
time. Each periodic coordinate is now wrapped exactly into (-p/2, p/2] and
mapped onto the circle of circumference p, as two coordinates whose
squared Euclidean distance is the squared chord, and one cdist takes every
distance: the trigonometric functions take one evaluation per point and
periodic dimension.

The kernels and their gradients agree with the previous computation to
about 2e-15 of the output variance over random inputs of all five ARD
kernels; inputs a whole number of periods apart are still the same input,
to the last bit. The cross-covariance of 100 x 2048 inputs of a
three-dimensional RQ-ARD kernel with two periodic dimensions takes 4.6 ms
instead of 15.2 (3.9 without periods), and the kernel with its gradient at
N = 200 1.5 ms instead of 4.0. A kernel without periods does not go
through this code and is unchanged.

Also: a test that the distance along a periodic dimension is the squared
chord, and AGENTS.md names the new helpers.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
